--TEST--
`Core\Out::capture` discards its callable's result, answers a value that outlives the next capture, closes its level on a caught throw, and runs `{through:}` after the level is already closed — spec § 12, `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/out.rs`'s module doc owns the three properties this
// case takes to depth; the neighbouring
// `out-capture-answers-the-sinks-carrier.nvst` pins the roster itself. What is
// here is the behaviour at each edge of the one member: what is answered, how
// long it lives, what happens on a throw, and when the transform runs.

// The member answers what was *written*, not what was computed: the callable's
// own return value is released and never reaches the caller. A `callable` is
// opaque as to signature (`rule:types/anonymous-function`), so a body that returns a `string` is an
// ordinary argument here rather than a type error.
echo "[", Core\Out::capture(fn (): string => { echo "written"; return "computed"; }), "]\n";

// The captured carrier is a value like any other: it is echoed twice, and the
// second capture does not disturb the first. Nothing about the capture stack
// leaks into what a closed capture answers.
var $first = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "one"; });
var $second = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "two"; });
echo "[", $first, $second, $first, "]\n";

// Nesting is call nesting three deep, and two captures that are siblings at
// the same level each take only their own writes — the second opens after the
// first has closed, so `d` is not part of `b<c>`.
echo "[", Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "a<";
    echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
        echo "b<";
        echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "c"; });
        echo ">";
    });
    echo "|";
    echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "d"; });
    echo ">";
}), "]\n";

// The level is closed on the throwing edge as well as the ordinary one, and
// this is where that matters: the throw is caught *inside* an enclosing
// capture, so if the inner level had stayed open the `after` below would have
// gone into an orphaned buffer instead of the outer one. What the inner
// capture had collected is dropped with it — a throw answers no carrier.
echo "[", Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "before|";
    try {
        echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "lost"; var $n = "abc" as int; echo $n; });
    } catch (Throwable $e) {
        echo "caught|";
    }
    echo "after";
}), "]\n";

// `{through:}` transforms the captured carrier, and it runs *after* the level
// has closed — so its own `echo` reaches the enclosing sink immediately, which
// is why `<in-through>` is written before the `body` this statement echoes on
// its way out. A transform is a transform, not an extra level of capture.
echo "[", Core\Out::capture(
    fn (): void => { echo "body"; },
    {through: fn (Core\Cli\Text $t): Core\Cli\Text => { echo "<in-through>"; return $t; }}
), "]\n";

// `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier` is why the carrier is not a `string`, so a `through` that hands
// back plain text is refused rather than quietly re-entering the sink. The
// message names the class the transform owed; the tag it answered with is left
// to the implementation.
try {
    echo Core\Out::capture(
        fn (): void => { echo "x"; },
        {through: fn (Core\Cli\Text $t): string => "plain"}
    );
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo (Core\Str::startsWith(
        $e->message,
        "`Core\\Out::capture`'s `through` must answer a `Core\\Cli\\Text`"
    ) ? "refused" : $e->message), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
[written]
[onetwoone]
[a<b<c>|d>]
[before|caught|after]
[<in-through>body]
refused
